    "name": "SQL Joins",
    "content": "Joins\n- The SQL JOIN statement is used to combine rows from two tables based\non a common column and selects records that have matching values in\nthese columns.\n- A join is an operation that combines the rows of two or more tables\nbased on related columns.\n\nJoins\n\nJoins\nThe result set will contain\nthose values where there is\na match between\ncustomer_id (of the\nCustomers table)\nand\ncustomer (of the Orders\ntable).\n\nJoins\n- A join is an operation that combines the rows of two or more tables\nbased on related (same type) columns.\n- This operation is used for retrieving the data from multiple tables\nsimultaneously using common columns of tables or columns that have\nsame datatype.\n- The main purpose of Join is to retrieve the data from multiple tables in\nother words Join is used to perform multi-table query. It is denoted by ⨝\nhere R1 and R2 are two relations to be join and R3\nis a relation that will holds the result of join\noperation.\n\nJoins\n\nJoins: Types\n- Inner Join\n- Equi Join\n- Natural Join\n- Outer join\n- Left outer join\n- Right outer join\n- Full outer join\n\nInner join\n- Inner Join is a join operation in DBMS that combines two or more table\nbased on related columns and return only rows that have matching\nvalues among tables.\n- Equi Join is a type of Inner join in which we use euivalence(‘=ʼ) condition\nin join condition\n\nInner join\n- Natural join is a type of inner join in which we not need of any\ncomparison operators. In natural join columns should have the same\nname and domain. There should be at least one common attribute\nbetween two tables.\n\nInner join\n- SQL joins the tables based on this common column and hence, we do not\nneed to explicitly specify the join condition\n- There is no need to use the ON clause for join condition in natural join.\n- There will always be unique columns in the output of a natural join.\n\nInner join\n\nInner join\n- We look for common column(s) in the employee and the department tables.\nThere is one column in both the tables which has the same name DeptID and\nthe same data type varchar.\n-Hence we use DeptID for joining our tables. SQL fetches the department ID of\neach employee from the employee table, maps the department ID to the\ncorresponding record in the department table and displays the output as a\nnew table with records merged from both the input tables.\n-Natural join is an intersection of tables based on a common column. It is also\nknown as natural inner join.\n\nInner join\n- Natural inner join only displays records with a common department ID. What\nif I want to include the departments which do not have an employee yet?\n-There are three types of natural join: Natural Left, Natural Right and Natural\nFull Join\n\nInner join\n-\nNatural left join displays every single record from the left table,\nirrespective of whether the common column value (here, DeptID) occurs in\nright table\nor not.is not present in department table. Hence the details\nThethe\ndepartment\nID D3000\nof Shreya whose department ID is D3000 were not available in the natural join output. In the\nabove example though, Shreya’s records are available since natural left join displays all the\nrecords from the left (employee) table.\n\nInner join\n- Every record from the right table will be present in the output. Suppose\nthere is a new Finance department but it does not have any employees yet.\nWe have an extra row for the Finance department with ID D4002, when compared to the\nnatural join example. Since no employee has joined this department yet, the employee details\nare all null.\n\nInner join\n- Natural full join is like a union of both input tables. If a given DeptID is not\navailable in the other table, the missing data will be filled with null values in\nthe output.\n\nInner join\n- We first create a natural join of the employee and department tables. Then\nwe filter out those records from the resultant table where the department\nname is Technology.\n\n1. J O I N S\nexplained\neasily\n2. Practice\n\nInner join\n\nLeft join\n\nLeft join\nActions not\nperformed by\nany user will\nnot\nbe\npresent in the\nresult\ntable\n(lets say a\nnew row in\nevent.action:\nlog out)\n\nRight join\n\nFull join\n\nUnion\n\nUnion\nDoes not ignore duplicates\nData types of columns must be\nsame\n\nCross Join\nCompares each rows from 1st table with each rows from 2nd table and\nlists serially\n\nCross Join\n\n1. J O I N S\nexplained\neasily\n2. Practice\n\nOuter join\n- Outer join is a type of join that retrieve matching as well as non-maching\nrecords from related tables.\n- Left outer join is also called left join. This type of outer join retrieve all\nrecords from left table and retrive maching record from right table.\n\nOuter join\n- Outer join is a type of join that retrieve matching as well as non-maching\nrecords from related tables.\n- Right outer join is also called right join. This type of outer join retrieve all\nrecords from right table and retrive maching record from left table.\n\nOuter join\n- In full outer join all the rows from both table are inserted in result table\n\nFull Outer join\n- .\n\nLeft Outer join\n\nLeft Outer join\nWe can observe that after Full\nouter Join,\n- for the course_ID 12, as there\nare no entries in the Student\ntable, its columns are null.\n- for the student_ID 3008 and\n3007, as there are no data in the\ncourse table, columns of the\ncourse table are null.\n- for the student_id 3002, as\nthere are two courses it appears\ntwice.\n\nLeft Outer join\n- Left outer join operation in SQL returns all the rows from the left, i.e., first\nrelation, and only matching rows from the right, i.e., second relation.\n- In case some row is present in the left table that is absent in the right table,\nthen values of rows of the right table are set to zero or null\n\nLeft Outer join\n\nRight Outer join\n- The right outer join operation in SQL returns all the rows from the right, i.e.,\nthe second relation and only matching rows from the left relation.\n- If some records from the right table don't match rows from the left table,\nthe null value is inserted in the columns of the left table.\n- The records that don't match in the left table are discarded.\n\nRight Outer join\n\nRight Outer join\n- In the above relation, all the\norders are included. The\ndetails of customers are\nfetched if it is available in\nthe customer table.\n- Else, the columns are\nkept NULL (in rows 4 and\n5). As we perform a right\nouter join, the customers\nnot having orders are\nexcluded, and customer\ndetails are included if\norder_id\nmatches\ntwo\ntables.\n- Self study – why use joins\n\nConclusion\n-Left outer join returns all the rows of the left table and only matches rows of\nthe right table.\n- Similarly, the Right outer join returns all the right table rows. Full\njoin returns all the matching rows of both tables.\n\nInner join\n\nThank You"
